Progressive Supranuclear Palsy
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Early backward falls, Vertical downward gaze palsy, Axial rigidity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-039
Reason for Visit: Recurrent, severe backward falls leading to a head strike and acute dysphagia preventing oral intake.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child (caregiver)
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a severe, unprovoked backward fall resulting in a head strike, complicated by severe choking on liquids today. Immediate assessment is required to rule out acute intracranial hemorrhage post-fall, assess airway safety due to severe dysphagia, and implement critical environmental safety measures before regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He just topples backward like a felled tree without any warning. He hit his head an hour ago, and now he chokes violently if he tries to drink water."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a known, progressive neurological syndrome. The hallmark is early, profound postural instability resulting in frequent, severe, and unprovoked backward falls. The caregiver reports symmetrical, severe axial rigidity prominently involving the neck and trunk far more than the limbs, with noticeable retrocollis (extended neck posture). The patient exhibits a distinctly startled, wide-eyed, and unblinking facial expression (procerus sign). There is progressive, severe pseudobulbar palsy directly causing profound dysphagia, severe dysarthria, and distinctly emotional lability (frequent unprovoked crying). The caregiver notes extreme apathy and distinct frontal lobe executive dysfunction. A previous trial of high-dose levodopa yielded an extremely poor response. Today, the patient suffered a severe backward fall, striking the occiput. Subsequently, the dysphagia has worsened acutely, with violent choking on water.
Functional Status: Highly dependent for all mobility due to extreme fall risk; requiring significant assistance with feeding.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of fatal aspiration or catastrophic traumatic brain injury from unprovoked backward falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/90 mmHg | HR: 82 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Sitting rigidly upright, startled wide-eyed expression, audible wet/gurgling respirations.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals the diagnostic tetrad: Early backward falls (by history), severe axial rigidity (unable to passively flex the neck), and a vertical supranuclear gaze palsy distinctly characterized by a marked inability to voluntarily look downward (when asked to look down at their toes, the eyes remain fixed forward; oculocephalic reflex overcomes this, confirming supranuclear origin). Gait is distinctly slow, broad-based, and severely unsteady, completely lacking the classic festination of Parkinsonism. Examination of the occiput reveals a 2cm boggy hematoma; no laceration, pupils equal and reactive, GCS 15. Bedside swallow assessment (teaspoon of water) immediately elicits severe coughing and pooling.
Environmental Observations: Hardwood floors without rugs; sharp-edged coffee table directly behind the patient's favorite chair.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil specific Parkinsonian medications (discontinued due to lack of efficacy).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Progressive Supranuclear Palsy - Status: Exacerbated (Acute trauma and severe dysphagia)
Differentials:

Subdural Hematoma (Post-fall) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: GCS remains 15, no fluctuating level of consciousness, pupils reactive, and no new unilateral focal motor signs, though the head strike warrants close clinical observation.

Parkinson's Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of vertical downward gaze palsy, severe axial (rather than appendicular) rigidity, early profound backward falls, and the documented lack of response to Levodopa.

Acute Aspiration Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile, lungs currently clear to auscultation at the bases (despite upper airway gurgling), though the risk is extremely high.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with acute trauma following a pathognomonic backward fall characteristic of Progressive Supranuclear Palsy, complicated by an acute, severe exacerbation of pseudobulbar dysphagia. Delaying assessment would expose the patient to a massive risk of an unobserved intracranial hemorrhage overnight or a fatal aspiration event if the caregiver attempted to force oral hydration. Urgent after-hours assessment was critical to clinically clear the patient of an acute, massive intracranial bleed, definitively establish strict nil by mouth (NBM) safety protocols to prevent aspiration, and immediately implement mechanical fall-prevention strategies to safeguard the patient until allied health could intervene.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised strict Nil By Mouth (NBM) status regarding thin fluids; prescribed commercial thickener for all necessary fluid intake. Provided head injury observation protocols to the caregiver.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Step 1: By mixing all drinks with a commercial thickener to a honey consistency, the caregiver slows the fluid transit in the throat, giving the weak swallowing muscles time to close the airway and preventing fatal choking.

Step 2: By positioning the patient's chair firmly against a solid wall, the caregiver physically prevents any possibility of a catastrophic backward fall when the patient attempts to stand up.

Step 3: By elevating the patient's food strictly to eye level rather than placing it on a low table, the caregiver completely bypasses the patient's inability to look down, immediately restoring their ability to see and eat their meal.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to Speech Pathology for formal modified barium swallow and Dietitian review.
DME/Safety (Optional): Weighted four-wheeled walker and high-backed, heavy armchair.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 48 hours for clinical review of the head injury and dysphagia status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call an ambulance immediately if the patient becomes increasingly drowsy, develops severe vomiting, or complains of a worsening headache (signs of a slow brain bleed). Call 000 if the patient turns blue or cannot clear their airway after choking.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The disease is relentlessly progressive; the medical focus is entirely on symptom management and profound safety and environmental modification.
Clinical Course: The head bump should resolve without issue if no red flags appear overnight. The swallowing difficulty and backward falls are permanent features of the disease and require strict, permanent lifestyle changes.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to give the patient any thin liquids (water, tea, coffee) without thickener. Ensure all food is pureed or very soft.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This specific condition damages the brain pathways that control downward eye movement, swallowing, and backward balance. Standard Parkinson's drugs do not work. He literally cannot look down to see his feet or his plate, and his swallowing muscles are uncoordinated. We must physically adapt his environment to keep him safe from falling and choking.
Actionable Steps: Push his chair against the wall right now. Use the fluid thickener for every single drink. Monitor his alertness closely tonight.

